(g)In I.T.C. Ltd. v. CCE&C (Appeals) ILR 2000 Kar. 25, the Karnataka High Court held as under :— "While considering the case of ‘undue hardship’, the authority is required to examine the prima facie on merits of the dispute as well. Pleading of financial disability would not be the only consideration. Where the case is fully covered in favour of the assessee by abiding precedent like that of the judgment of the Supreme Court, jurisdictional High Court or a Special Bench of the Tribunal, then to still insist upon the deposit of duty and penalty levied would certainly cause undue hardship to the assessee. Absence of the financial hardship in such a case would be no ground to decline the dispensation of pre-deposit under the proviso to section 35F. The power to dispense with such deposit is conferred under the authorities has to be exercised precisely in cases like this type and if it is not exercised under such circumstances then this Court will require it to be exercised. Such like cases where two views are not possible then the condition of pre-deposit before the appeal is heard on merits, can be dispensed with. In case two views are possible on interpretation, based on conflicting judgment of the jurisdictional High Court then the authorities may pass the order under proviso to section 35F of the Act keeping in view the facts of the case in hand.

(h)In LG Electronics India (P.) Ltd. v. CCE [2009] 22 STT 269 (All.), the appellant was issued with a show-cause notice as to why—

uEducation cess amounting to Rs. 3,58,717 short/not paid by it and interest accruing thereon, should not be demanded and recovered from it respectively, under sections 11A and 11AB of the Central Excise Act, 1944 and the amount of Rs. 2,54,805 already paid should not be appropriated and adjusted against the total amount so payable;

uPenalty under rule 25 of the Central Excise Rules, 2002 read with section 11AC of the Central Excise Act, 1944 should not be imposed upon it;

uCenvat credit amounting to Rs. 6,21,39,585 wrongly availed and utilized should not be demanded and recovered from it under rule 14 of the Cenvat Credit Rules, 2004 read with section 11A of the Central Excise Act, 1944;

uInterest should not be recovered from it under rule 14 of the Cenvat Credit Rules, 2004 on the amount of credit wrongly availed and utilized read with section 11AC of the Central Excise Act, 1944; and

uPenalty under rule 15 of the Cenvat Credit Rules, 2004 read with section 11AC should not be imposed on it for the amount of credit wrongly availed and utilized by it.

The appellant replied to the show-cause notice contesting the allegations made in the show-cause notice on diverse grounds that the demand was barred by time and the question of eligibility of a manufacturer to avail Cenvat credit against outward transportation of goods having been referred to a Larger Bench, the proceedings may be kept in abeyance, till adjudication. The Commissioner of Central Excise confirmed the proposal contained in the show-cause notice.

The appellant, aggrieved with the order, preferred an appeal before the Tribunal with a prayer for waiver of pre-deposit of any part of the duty and the penalty imposed would cause undue hardship and irreparable loss. The Tribunal directed to deposit a sum of Rs. 1 crore within eight weeks and subject to said deposit being made, the balance amount of the duty and the penalty was waived and recovery thereof was stayed till the disposal of the appeal. Therefore, the appellant filed this present appeal before the High Court.

The appellant submitted the following :

u If the appellant has a strong prima facie case on merit, the appellate authority is bound to examine the issue and if prima facie, a strong case on merit is established, the condition of pre-deposit ought to be waived;

u Financial hardship does not mean to find out as to whether the appellant is in a position to make the payment or not, but it means that the Tribunal is under an obligation to examine prima facie case whether the appellant is liable to make any payment at all and if the case on merit is prima facie found to be established, waiver should be permitted.

The department submitted the following :

u The appellant-company was a profit-making company and it had means to deposit a sum of Rs. 1 crore as directed by the Tribunal as pre-deposit;

u There was no reason to interfere with the impugned order;

u In case the appellant succeeded before the Tribunal, the amount of pre-deposit was liable to be refunded with interest; as such the order did not require any interference;

