Section 203AA

Finance Act, 2006

Credit for TDS and TCS - Dematerialisation postponed by two more years

8.2 An assessee is entitled to credit in respect of tax deducted at source ('TDS') and tax collected at source ('TCS') on the basis of a certificate issued by the person responsible for deducting or collecting tax at source. Such certificate has to be enclosed with the return of income, to claim the credit.

The Finance (No.2) Act, 2004 proposed dematerialisation of TDS and TCS certificates and, to state briefly1, provided that on and after 1-4-2005:

the person responsible for deducting or collecting tax at source should file quarterly statements with the income-tax authority or authorised person;

such authority or person would furnish a statement of tax deducted or collected at source ('the Statement') to the payee or buyer;

the person responsible for deducting or collecting tax at source need not furnish certificate to the payee or buyer; and

the payee or buyer would be entitled to the credit for TDS or TCS based on the statement furnished by such authority or person.

The Finance Act, 2005 postponed the implementation of the above by a year.

Pending stabilisation of the system, the Finance Act, 2006 has amended, with effect from 1-4-2006, that is, assessment year 2006-07, the following provisions to provide that the amendments made therein by the Finance (No. 2) Act, 2004, as further amended by the Finance Act, 2005 would become effective from 1-4-2008 instead of 1-4-2006 as provided heretobefore:

Section 199(3) of the Act providing for credit on the basis of the Statement;

Section 203(3) of the Act providing that a payer need not issue a certificate of TDS;

Section 203AA of the Act providing that the prescribed authority shall furnish the statement of TDS;

Proviso to section 206C(4) of the Act providing for credit on the basis of the statement;

First proviso to section 206C(5) providing that a person responsible for collecting tax at source need not issue a certificate of TCS;

Second proviso to section 206C(5) providing that the prescribed authority shall furnish the statement of TCS; and

Explanation below section 139(9) providing that if the proof of tax deducted or collected at source is not attached after 1-4-2008, the return would not be considered as defective.

The effect of the above amendments is as follows:

the credit for TDS or TCS, in respect of the financial years 2005-06 and 2006-07 (assessment years 2006-07 and 2007-08), will be available on the basis of certificates issued by the payer or the person responsible for collecting tax at source;

the payee or the buyer will have to attach the proof of TDS or TCS along with the return of income for assessment years 2006-07 and 2007-08, otherwise, the return would be considered as defective under Explanation below section 139(9) of the Act; and

the person responsible for deducting or collecting tax at source will have to continue to issue certificates to the payee or the buyer, in respect of the tax deducted or collected at source during the financial years 2005-06 and 2006-07.

